    "narrative": "For today\u2019s slate, our lineup leans heavily into the scarcity of Stolen Bases, prioritizing green lights against slower deliveries. **Corbin Carroll** (76.69) stands out as our premier play; he benefits from a favorable matchup against Pallante, and with the \"Slow SP\" factor in play, his elite sprint speed gives him significant upside in the SB category. **Jose Caballero** (74.46) is nearly as potent, riding a massive +13% platoon boost that vaults his efficiency score into the elite tier despite a matchup against a tough lefty in Valdez. **Brice Turang** (73.69) rounds out our top trio, aided by a wind-out scenario at the park and a generous SP xERA of 4.45 from Singer, making him an ideal anchor for our middle infield.\n\nThe most difficult decision on the roster involves the utility spot, where **Kyle Karros** (59.90) narrowly edged out a higher-ceiling prospect profile by capitalizing on a 14% park factor boost and a favorable platoon matchup against Bennett. While **Austin Riley** (57.02) and **Gabriel Moreno** (58.21) sit just below the 60-point \"Strong\" threshold, they remain essential starts given our limited depth and the need to maximize our daily caps. We remain wary of **Zach McKinstry** (46.30), whose score is suppressed by a negative platoon differential against Cole; he stays in the lineup only because of the scarcity of options, but he carries the highest \"below floor\" risk on the team today.\n\nWe continue to monitor the status of **Josh Naylor**, **Mickey Moniak**, **Trevor Story**, and **Giancarlo Stanton**, all of whom remain unavailable due to off-days or IL designations. With our bench currently devoid of healthy active contributors like **Vinnie Pasquantino**, **Tyler Soderstrom**, **Max Muncy**, **Carson Williams**, **Cole Young**, or **Henry Bolte**, we are essentially running our primary core at full capacity. Should any of the \"Lineup Pending\" designations shift closer to first pitch, we will need to re-evaluate **Dalton Rushing** and **Alex Freeland**, as their current sub-50 scores are highly sensitive to batting order fluctuations; a drop further down the order could necessitate a shift in our late-game strategy to preserve our total game caps.",
